This paper examines a specific class of manifolds, denoted as $\mathcal{M}$, which are complete, noncompact, and possess nonnegative Ricci curvature. We demonstrate that within these manifolds, solutions exist for the following problem:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{cases}
-\text{div}\left(\frac{1}{|x|^{\alpha(p_1)}}\left(\frac{1}{|x|^{\beta(p_2)}}\right)^{p_1}\nabla u\right)^{\alpha(p_1)}\left(\frac{1}{|x|^{\beta(p_2)}}\right)^{\alpha(p_1)}\left(\frac{1}{|x|^{\beta(p_2)}}\right)^{p_2}u^{p_2} = \lambda u^q, & x \in \mathcal{M} \setminus \{0\}, \\
u(0) = 0, & x \in \mathcal{M} \setminus \{0\},
\end{cases}
\end{equation*}
where the exponents $p_1, p_2, q$ are real numbers, and the functions $\alpha, \beta$ are defined on $\mathbb{R}^N \setminus \{0\}$.
